<title>GERMANY: Ivorian main crop handling aided by drought - trader.</title>
<headline>Ivorian main crop handling aided by drought - trader.</headline>
<dateline>HAMBURG 1997-02-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Drought damage to the Ivorian mid-crop may be more than offset by the benefits that dry weather brought to the handling of the main crop, German trader Albrecht &amp; Dill said in a weekly market report.</p>
<p>A&amp;D agreed with the top range of current 1996/97 Ivorian crop estimates at 1.1 million tonnes (1.2 million in 1995/96). Official projections limit the upside to one million tonnes.</p>
<p>Recent rains relieved the stress on the mid-crop created by drought earlier this month, which industry sources say may limit the country's overall harvest in 1996/97.</p>
<p>But A&amp;D said the earlier dry weather had speeded up the drying, processing and transport of the main crop.</p>
<p>A&amp;D also said German cocoa processors were drawing down raw materials stocks and were looking to buy at levels four or five percent below the current market.</p>
<p>--Hamburg newsroom +49-40-41903275</p>
